#### Problem 2: Service Overload Creates Cascading Failures

**Scenario**: During peak load (8AM-10AM), eligibility service returns 503 errors. Calling services immediately fail requests, amplifying the problem.

**Traditional Approach (Immediate Failure)**:

```csharp
public class EligibilityApiClient
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;

    public async Task<Eligibility> CheckEligibilityAsync(Guid memberId, DateTime serviceDate)
    {
        var response = await _httpClient.GetAsync(
            $"https://eligibility-api.gainwell.com/members/{memberId}/eligibility?date={serviceDate:yyyy-MM-dd}");

        if (response.StatusCode == HttpStatusCode.ServiceUnavailable)
        {
            // 503 Service Unavailable - fail immediately
            throw new ServiceUnavailableException("Eligibility service temporarily unavailable");

            // Problem: No retry logic means all requests during overload period fail
            // Multiple calling services fail simultaneously
            // User-facing systems show "system unavailable" errors
            // Operations team receives thousands of alerts
        }

        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<Eligibility>();
    }
}
```

**Observed Behavior (Gainwell Production Incident - 2024-09-15)**:
- **Eligibility service overloaded**: CPU 95%, returning 503 errors
- **Claims adjudication service**: 15,000 failed requests in 10 minutes (no retry logic)
- **Provider portal**: "System unavailable" errors for all eligibility checks
- **Recovery time**: 45 minutes (manual service restart, connection pool reset)
- **Claims backlog**: 22,000 claims requiring manual reprocessing

**Root Cause**: Temporary overload (caused by batch job running during peak hours) created cascading failures across dependent services. No retry logic meant temporary 503 errors became permanent request failures.

